WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.880
Hey guys, quick note before we get into the&nbsp;
video. I've been pretty busy moving between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.640
states and working on my startup, Silo. So, my&nbsp;
brother is filling in for me on this video. Let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.280
me know what you think in the comments. With&nbsp;
that out of the way, let's get right into it.&nbsp;

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:21.440
The rental car business just had its&nbsp;
best quarter in years… except for Hertz.&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.840
And the reason is pretty wild.&nbsp;

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.840
Their CEO said "Electric&nbsp;
vehicles are now mainstream."&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.600
Then soon after, dumped tens of&nbsp;
thousands of EVs, at a low price.&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.120
But, things have gotten so much worse, and&nbsp;
it seems like it’s entirely their fault.&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.880
EVs are now the saving grace&nbsp;
for the rental industry…&nbsp;

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.560
Hertz are also being sued&nbsp;
for misleading shareholders.&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:53.680
And now, their AI will bill customers for&nbsp;
fake damage… without any human review.&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.360
Everything keeps getting&nbsp;
worse and worse for Hertz.&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:09.360
So, how did it all happen?
How did things go from bad to worse?&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:14.640
Okay, let’s start with those EVs.
After that… bold announcement from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:21.600
CEO Mark Fields, Hertz announced it was going&nbsp;
to add 100,000 Teslas to their fleet. And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:28.320
was just by the end of 2022. Of course,&nbsp;
the real number they got was about 35,000.&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:34.320
They also planned to buy 65,000 Polestar EVs&nbsp;
over five years, and were even planning to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:41.120
add models from GM's electric brands later on.
A bold move, but Hertz needed one. They had just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:47.040
emerged from bankruptcy the previous year.
They also did this for a few reasons.&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:52.880
First, they thought this would lower costs.
Gasoline prices were high and volatile.&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.840
Not only that, EVs promised significantly&nbsp;
lower overall maintenance costs.&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:04.240
No oil changes, no complex transmissions, and&nbsp;
fewer parts to break down meant less time in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:10.000
shop and more time on the road making money.
At least, that's how it seemed…&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.680
But there’s more.
The industry, at least at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:21.600
was giving clear signals that EVs were the future.
Tesla had just passed a $1 trillion market cap.&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.400
Their profits were skyrocketing.
The legacy automakers were now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:32.640
pouring billions into battery factories.
So, it seemed that EVs were now mainstream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.280
to quote CEO Mark Fields.
But, things seemed to backfire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:42.400
in almost every way.
EVs don't need oil changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.480
but they have other problems.
Hertz found that its repair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.400
costs were actually 20% higher than for&nbsp;
comparable internal combustion vehicles.&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:58.000
If a Tesla gets in a minor collision, it often&nbsp;
requires specialized, expensive parts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.840
labor that only the manufacturer can provide.
Data showed that the maintenance and repair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:09.520
costs for EVs were significantly higher&nbsp;
than for the gasoline cars in its fleet.&nbsp;

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:14.160
Plus while repair costs&nbsp;
were high, adoption was low.&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.520
Range anxiety, the lack of&nbsp;
charging infrastructure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:24.000
plus vacations often involve long-distance&nbsp;
driving, which is a weakness of EV cars.&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.120
And this, with the high repair costs,&nbsp;
contributed to an even bigger problem.&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.320
Hertz bought tens of thousands of EVs, expecting&nbsp;
them to hold their value well in the used car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:39.600
market after their rental life was over.
But the opposite happened.&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.480
Due to a multitude of factors, like&nbsp;
soft demand, a trade war with China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:50.480
as well as an influx of cheaper EVs from&nbsp;
China, prices for EVs began to come down.&nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:56.560
Ford had to cut prices of the F-150&nbsp;
Lightning by as much as $7,000.&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:03.680
So what happens when the price of a brand-new car&nbsp;
drops by $7,000? It hammers the resale value of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.640
every used model already on the market.
With all this chaos happening, just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.600
imagine what was happening for Hertz.
Suddenly, the "asset value" part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:21.760
Hertz's EV bet looked far worse than for gas cars.
Okay, so their EVs are worth less. So what?&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:25.520
Well, rental car companies&nbsp;
work a little differently.&nbsp;

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:30.000
Rental companies like Hertz&nbsp;
use asset-backed securities.&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.080
If Hertz wants a $30,000 car, they borrow $30,000&nbsp;
against it. The bank's security is the car itself.&nbsp;

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:43.280
While holding that car, they’ll rent it out,&nbsp;
let’s say they make $18,000, while paying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:49.840
off that 30k, with its interest.
Then sell it for maybe $22,000.&nbsp;

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:54.480
They repay the bank, but they made&nbsp;
good rental income, so they profit.&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.520
There’s operating costs, and interest of&nbsp;
course, but the main thing is rental cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:06.240
are less assets, and more, backed securities.
Hertz isn't really a company that owns half a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:12.320
million cars. It's a company that operates&nbsp;
half a million cars owned by bondholders.&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:18.160
So… if used-car prices drop, that’s a bigger&nbsp;
difference Hertz has to pay its lenders.&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.800
But Hertz's fleet was massive.
So just a 1% drop in used-car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:29.360
values cost Hertz about $75 million.
This is why they initially went bankrupt.&nbsp;

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:33.360
And when the pandemic killed travel&nbsp;
and briefly tanked used-car prices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.160
Hertz couldn't keep up with those payments.
But now, it was happening all over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.440
again. Just with EVs.
Hertz also saw $245 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:50.320
in charges related to depreciation expenses from&nbsp;
the EV sales. In just the fourth quarter of 2023.&nbsp;

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:56.000
Hertz began selling them off.
20,000 of them, many of which were Teslas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:01.360
just 2 years after ordering them.
Many were barely used Tesla Model 3s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:08.160
listed as low as $20,000, roughly half of&nbsp;
what it had paid for them a year prior.&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:13.920
The depreciation hit was so severe that&nbsp;
Hertz had to become… creative in unloading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.920
its excess EV inventory.
Hertz began asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:24.240
renters if they wanted to buy the EVs.
For example, Hertz offered one customer a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:35.280
2023 Chevrolet Bolt for about $18,400, and another&nbsp;
a barely year-old Tesla Model 3 for $17,900.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:40.480
This was bad. But, things&nbsp;
have gotten so much worse.&nbsp;

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:43.200
Way worse.
This move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:50.880
while it may have been smart at the time, has&nbsp;
put Hertz in an even worse position, as EVs… have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:58.080
ironically become the saving grace of rentals?
Now, Hertz are being sued by investors… but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:02.240
not for selling the EVs.
Something much worse.&nbsp;

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:07.840
But, before we get to that, we need to talk&nbsp;
about another new technology, Hertz embraced.&nbsp;

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:12.480
Since EVs had gone so well. (sarcasm).
We have to talk about Hertz’s strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:22.240
experiment… with AI.
AI has undoubtedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:26.960
changed at least one industry: coding.
But, this has created a new bottleneck.&nbsp;

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:30.480
There's more AI code than ever,&nbsp;
which means devs now spend more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.560
time reviewing code than writing it.
And reviewing 30,000 lines of code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:38.320
by hand isn't realistic either.
But a generic agent doesn't fix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:43.760
it. It can quickly lose context on past pull&nbsp;
requests, comments, changes, or discussion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:48.320
that happened in Slack, outside the code.
