00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:06.720
In 2025, Hertz entered a maintenance partnership&nbsp;
with UVeye, worth tens of millions per year.&nbsp;

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:11.840
They said this would be “transforming&nbsp;
its vehicle maintenance process”.&nbsp;

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:18.320
And soon, customers began to experience&nbsp;
the uh… “fruit” of this partnership.&nbsp;

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:22.800
Jessica Fields returned her&nbsp;
Hertz car to the Atlanta airport.&nbsp;

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:30.800
She’s a Hertz Gold member, so, a loyal customer.
But, 17 minutes later, she got a notification,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:35.920
saying damage was detected.
“I was really mostly confused because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:42.480
didn’t see any damage when I dropped off the car,”
She returned, and asked to see the car. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:48.400
Hertz staff refused.
She asked for a manager… she was declined.&nbsp;

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:53.360
So she chased up Hertz again and again,&nbsp;
and she was finally given pictures of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:58.320
the damage… as well as the charge.
Do you see the terrible state she left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:02.400
the car in?… Yeah me neither.
Apparently there were two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:08.560
minuscule dents, in those orange squares.
Fields didn’t see any damage when she dropped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:15.040
it off, and it sure looks like there’s no damage.
But then, she got the bill.&nbsp;

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:23.280
$160 for the dents… and $190&nbsp;
in administrative fees. $190!&nbsp;

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:27.440
$350 total.
When she tried to dispute the charges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:33.200
the email link mysteriously didn’t work.
Thing is, Hertz aren’t the only ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:39.120
using AI to detect damages.
But… Hertz are a bit special.&nbsp;

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:45.440
“Some other car rental companies reportedly&nbsp;
use AI as a tool but require human staff to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:51.840
review before billing customers; however, Hertz&nbsp;
is apparently the only car rental company in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:56.880
U.S. that issues damage assessments&nbsp;
to customers without human review.”&nbsp;

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:00.400
Yeah.
So, that $190 for administration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:06.800
is basically free revenue for Hertz. Because&nbsp;
it’s not like a human went and looked at it.&nbsp;

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:11.760
It’s not just Fields either
Hertz said that fewer than 3% of vehicles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:18.080
scanned by the system are billed for damages.
Though, whether that 3% actually have damaged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:22.720
vehicles is another story.
You could see Hertz’s focus.&nbsp;

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:30.480
Future technology maybe, but primarily: Ways&nbsp;
to cut down costs, by removing areas of labor.&nbsp;

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:34.720
And maybe implicitly, get&nbsp;
more money out of customers.&nbsp;

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:38.640
But, here’s where the story&nbsp;
gets a bit… complicated.&nbsp;

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:46.160
Revenue for Hertz is down&nbsp;
6%. 2024 was also down 3.44%.&nbsp;

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:49.280
But, they’re now making way more money.&nbsp;
(highlight net income and gross income growth).&nbsp;

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:56.400
And, in Q2 2026, they had a&nbsp;
GAAP net income of $64 million.&nbsp;

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:02.320
At least, that’s the way it seems.
In reality, things couldn’t be worse for Hertz.&nbsp;

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:09.280
They are currently drowning in debt, and despite&nbsp;
promising they had strong cash to survive… just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:14.480
few weeks later had to go and raise more debt.
Now, they’re being sued.&nbsp;

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:20.960
Plus, there’s the most ironic part of all&nbsp;
of this: The EVs they sold, which turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:31.200
out to be a saving grace.
Just not for Hertz.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:35.040
If you’ve watched this far, consider&nbsp;
subscribing! It helps the channel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:43.920
and means you won’t miss future videos.
In Q4 2023, Hertz had $245 million of depreciation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:50.160
charges that quarter alone. So they sold.
After selling them off en masse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:55.920
at a huge discount, EVs are now the&nbsp;
best performing segment in the market.&nbsp;

00:12:55.920 --> 00:13:05.600
In 2026, the EV index was up 12% year&nbsp;
over year, and non-EVs were up just 1.7%.&nbsp;

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:12.000
So… Hertz bought the peak with EVs,&nbsp;
sold at the bottom, before EVs spiked.&nbsp;

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:15.120
Nice.
For those still holding EVs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:22.320
their financials are much better, and their&nbsp;
asset value is up, so depreciation cost is down.&nbsp;

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:26.800
But, Hertz didn't get those&nbsp;
benefits, and potentially even worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:32.640
their stated reason for selling, was wrong.&nbsp;
They sold because they thought EVs wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:38.000
hold value, and now it’s the opposite.
Of course, the market could change again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:43.520
but the timing for Hertz couldn’t be worse.
And it gets worse.&nbsp;

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:50.960
In mid-2026 Hertz stated “current unexpected&nbsp;
softness in the used car market” caused losses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:57.280
and caused them to adjust their EBITDA forecast.
But… that doesn’t seem to be the case.&nbsp;

00:13:57.280 --> 00:14:04.000
“Cox Automotive's Manheim index tells a&nbsp;
different story: the MUVVI reached 212.9&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:11.200
in June, up 2.1% year over year”.
And remember, EVs were up 12%.&nbsp;

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:17.040
Fairly suspicious, which brings us to an&nbsp;
even bigger problem they have right now.&nbsp;

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:22.640
After emerging from their 2020&nbsp;
bankruptcy, Hertz had lost a lot of money.&nbsp;

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:31.520
A net loss of $747 million in 2025,&nbsp;
and a $2.8 billion loss in 2024.&nbsp;

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:38.480
Hertz had no money, and they owed a lot.
Hertz has two giant debt piles. The vehicle debt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:45.600
which we talked about in chapter 1, is about&nbsp;
$12.7 billion — car loans backed by cars.&nbsp;

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:50.160
But also, another, non-vehicle&nbsp;
debt. Corporate debt.&nbsp;

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:56.160
Total debt is 18 billion, which makes&nbsp;
their interest expenses pretty big.&nbsp;

00:14:56.160 --> 00:15:04.480
259 million just for Q2 2026. And&nbsp;
for the first 6 months, 515 million.&nbsp;

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:08.800
As for Q2 2026, that GAAP net income of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:15.200
$64 million, wasn’t really from the business.
[Scott Haralson, CFO]: “GAAP net income benefited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:22.400
from gains on the sale of real estate locations&nbsp;
on which we completed sale-leaseback transactions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:29.040
as well as revaluations of other exchangeable&nbsp;
notes and warrants issued in prior years”&nbsp;

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:36.160
Adjusted net loss was actually $47 million.
So, they don’t have money, and have a ton of debt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:42.000
which meant a ton of interest, which it’s&nbsp;
already paying with debt, or other means.&nbsp;

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:46.480
So, the question became: how long&nbsp;
can this company keep operating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:52.720
before it needs to ask someone for money?
Which brings us to their Q1 earnings call,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:56.480
and Hertz’s liquidity.
The reason they’re being sued,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.480 --> 00:16:01.280
for misleading shareholders.
In the call, CFO Scott Haralson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.280 --> 00:16:10.400
said liquidity was $837 million, expected to&nbsp;
reach just under $1 billion by the end of Q2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:16.800
and "north of $1.5 billion" by year end.
This is basically the cash they have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:21.360
and what they can draw immediately.
Haralson was basically saying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:27.600
Hertz has a long runway, and can safely pay their&nbsp;
operating costs and obligations for a good while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:31.360
without taking on more debt…
But just six weeks later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:37.040
after promising liquidity was good,&nbsp;
Hertz, you guessed it, needed money.&nbsp;

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:40.640
But there was a problem.
No one wants to lend to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:46.080
company like Hertz, they’re not doing&nbsp;
well. So, they did something else.&nbsp;

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:52.960
They announced a three hundred and fifty&nbsp;
million dollar bond, at 6.75% interest.&nbsp;

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:58.240
They then cut their profit forecast to&nbsp;
somewhere between fifty and eighty million.&nbsp;

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:04.560
To get that 350 million, they issued new&nbsp;
shares, 37 million of them, and lent them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:10.240
to JPMorgan Chase, and this is crazy.
“one of its affiliates intends to sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:14.960
the Borrowed Shares and use the resulting&nbsp;
short position to facilitate transactions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:19.440
by which investors in the Notes.
The activity described above could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:24.880
affect the market price of the Common Stock”.
Basically, Hertz lent them shares to short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.880 --> 00:17:31.040
aka betting against Hertz’s stock, which&nbsp;
Hertz doesn’t receive proceeds from.&nbsp;

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:36.160
Not only was their liquidity promise, just&nbsp;
not true, but to get more capital, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:42.640
had to let someone bet against their own stock.
Even worse… there wasn’t enough stock around to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:49.760
borrow to do so. Roughly 30% of the tradable&nbsp;
stock is sold short, about 10 times what a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:52.960
normal company carries.
They just made more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:59.040
diluting existing shareholders.
So, you can probably guess what happened next.&nbsp;

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:04.400
The allegation is that Hertz told investors the&nbsp;
money was fine when it knew this was coming.&nbsp;

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:08.800
Strangely enough, the lawsuit named the&nbsp;
defendants as the company, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:15.760
but also CEO Gil West, and CFO Scott Haralson.
The most ironic thing, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:22.480
is that Hertz technically hit their goal.
It landed at $984 million in liquidity.&nbsp;

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:27.520
Just, to GET that liquidity, it needed&nbsp;
more debt, and to dilute shares.&nbsp;

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:33.120
It’s not a bad time to be a rental car&nbsp;
company, but boy is it a bad time to be Hertz.&nbsp;

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:37.840
They’re barely a company at this point, and&nbsp;
more a debt and interest paying machine.&nbsp;

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:43.040
Little profit, high liabilities.
Will they collapse? Who knows.&nbsp;

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:48.560
But their current state might be even worse.
Hertz isn't the only car company in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:51.200
a strange position.
You might recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:56.400
this. The Carvana vending machine.
A used car dealership which somehow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.400 --> 00:19:00.880
ended up on the S&amp;P 500.
And, the whole thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:05.760
might be a gigantic pump &amp; dump.
